    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The honest answer is that mileage matters less than maintenance. Two identical machines
          with the same hours on them can be five years apart in belt condition, and the variable
          is whether anyone ever lubricated the deck underneath it.
        </p>

        <div id="typical-lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Typical Lifespan</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A well-maintained home treadmill belt lasts five to ten years</strong>, or
            roughly 1,500 to 3,000 hours of use. For a machine used three times a week for
            45 minutes that is comfortably a decade, and many owners never replace a belt at all
            before replacing the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A neglected belt can need replacing in two to three years.</strong> The
            mechanism is straightforward: without lubrication the belt runs against a dry deck,
            friction rises, and the fabric backing abrades away far faster than it would otherwise.
            Heat from that friction also degrades the bond between the belt's layers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial-grade belts</strong> on machines built for gym duty cycles are
            thicker and typically last considerably longer in a home setting — often the life of
            the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            The useful framing is that belt replacement is normal wear rather than failure, in the
            way tyres are. What is not normal is needing one at year three, and that is always a
            maintenance story rather than a quality one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="five-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Five Signs a Belt Is Due</h2>
<p>
            <strong>One: visible thinning or shine down the centre.</strong> Look along the belt in
            raking light. A distinct polished strip where your feet land, noticeably smoother than
            the edges, means the top surface is worn. Compare the centre against the outer inches
            that never take load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two: fraying or curling at the edges.</strong> Some edge fuzz is normal over
            years. Actual fraying, or edges that have started to curl upward, means the structure
            is going.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three: the layers separating.</strong> The running surface lifting away from the
            fabric backing anywhere, usually starting at an edge or a seam. This is the one sign
            that means replace now rather than plan to — a delaminating belt can fail suddenly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four: slipping that lubrication and tension do not fix.</strong> If you have
            lubricated properly and set tension correctly and the belt still hesitates under load,
            the backing has likely lost its low-friction properties.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five: a rough or gritty feel on the underside.</strong> Run a hand along the
            backing with the belt slack. It should feel smooth. A texture like fine sandpaper means
            the backing has abraded and it is now working against the deck rather than sliding on
            it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How to Check Properly</h2>
<p>
            Ten minutes, once a year, ideally as part of the same session in which you clean under
            the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Unplug the machine.</strong> Then loosen both rear roller bolts by one full turn
            each, counting, so you can lift the belt and rotate it by hand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Inspect the top surface</strong> in raking light along the full length, looking
            for the polished centre strip and comparing it to the untrodden edges.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rotate the belt by hand</strong> and inspect the underside section by section,
            feeling for roughness and looking for wear-through of the backing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check both edges</strong> along the full length for fraying, curling or any
            separation of the layers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the deck at the same time.</strong> A polished or grooved deck surface
            usually accompanies a worn belt, and knowing both conditions is what tells you whether
            to replace one part or two. Our
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to cleaning under the belt</a>
            covers getting access safely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-accelerates-wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Makes Belts Wear Out Early</h2>
<p>
            <strong>No lubrication.</strong> By a wide margin the largest factor. A dry belt-deck
            interface is abrasive on every rotation, and it is why a belt can go from a decade to
            two years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incorrect tension.</strong> Over-tightening presses the belt into the deck and
            increases contact friction, wearing both faster while loading the rollers, bearings and
            motor. Under-tensioning lets it slip, which generates heat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Poor tracking.</strong> A belt running off-centre rubs against the frame or the
            side rails, which destroys an edge quickly. This is the most avoidable cause on the
            list and the one most likely to go unnoticed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Grit under the belt.</strong> Carpet fibre and household dust act as an abrasive
            between belt and deck, which is why machines on carpet wear belts faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Outdoor shoes.</strong> Grit carried in on soles is deposited directly on the
            running surface and then dragged around the rollers. Indoor-only shoes are a free
            extension of belt life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Higher user weight and running rather than walking</strong> both increase
            contact load, though these are inherent to use rather than errors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-and-deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Why the Deck Usually Goes With It</h2>
<p>
            Belt and deck wear as a pair, and replacing one without assessing the other is a common
            and expensive error.
          </p>
          <p>
            A worn belt with an abraded backing has been working against the deck for months,
            polishing away its low-friction laminate. Fitting a new belt onto a worn deck puts a
            fresh, correctly-textured backing against a surface that no longer slides well — so the
            new belt wears prematurely and you buy another sooner than you should.
          </p>
          <p>
            The good news is that <strong>many decks are reversible</strong>. The underside is an
            unused low-friction surface, and flipping the deck effectively gives you a second deck
            for the cost of labour alone. Since the machine has to come apart for a belt change
            anyway, the labour overlaps almost entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the sensible approach when a belt is due: assess the deck at the same time, and if it
            is polished or grooved, flip it — or replace it if it has already been flipped. Doing
            both together typically costs $250 to $500 all in and resets the wear items completely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What It Costs</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Belt part: $80-$250.</strong> Consumer machines sit at the lower end;
            commercial-grade and wide belts at the upper.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck part: $100-$300</strong>, if it needs replacing rather than flipping.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Labour: $100-$200</strong> for a belt change, and little more if the deck is
            flipped at the same time since the machine is already apart.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Service call-out: $80-$150</strong> on top, at many companies.
          </p>
          <p>
            So a professional belt change runs roughly <strong>$260 to $500</strong>, and belt plus
            deck together <strong>$300 to $650</strong>. Done yourself, it is the part cost alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set that against the machine's value before proceeding. On a $500 budget treadmill at
            year six, a $400 professional belt-and-deck job is most of a replacement machine and
            rarely worth it. On a $1,800 machine at year seven, it is straightforwardly good value.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            covers the repair-or-replace threshold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Can You Do It Yourself?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, for most people. A belt change is mechanically simple and physically awkward rather
            than technically demanding, and it does not involve the motor compartment or any
            electrical work.
          </p>
          <p>
            The job is: unplug, remove the motor hood and the rear end caps, fully release belt
            tension, remove the front roller, slide the old belt off, slide the new one on, refit
            the roller, retension, then set tracking. Two to three hours the first time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What makes it awkward</strong> rather than hard: the belt is heavy and stiff,
            the front roller is often tight in its brackets, and getting tension even across both
            sides takes patience. A second person makes it considerably easier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do it yourself if</strong> you are comfortable with basic tools, have someone to
            help, and the machine is out of warranty. <strong>Pay someone if</strong> the machine is
            under warranty, you cannot source the correct part with confidence, or the deck also
            needs attention and you would rather it were done once, properly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever route, the last step matters most: set tension with the lift test and then
            check tracking on a slow unloaded run. Our
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tension guide</a>
            and <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring guide</a>
            cover both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Choosing a Replacement Belt</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Get the exact size.</strong> Belts are specified by length and width, and both
            must match. Do not measure the old belt while it is fitted and under tension — use the
            manufacturer's part specification for your model and model year, because dimensions
            change between years under the same product name.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Match the thickness and construction.</strong> One-ply and two-ply belts behave
            differently. A thicker belt than the machine was designed for adds load to the motor and
            can foul the deck clearance; a thinner one wears faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Manufacturer or reputable third party.</strong> Third-party belts are usually
            fine and often cheaper, provided the supplier lists your exact model rather than a
            generic size. Avoid unbranded belts sold only by dimensions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ask whether the deck is reversible</strong> while you are ordering. It is a
            two-minute question that can save $100 to $300, and it is not something a parts seller
            will volunteer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="if-you-dont" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Happens If You Leave It</h2>
<p>
            Running on a worn belt is not immediately dangerous in most cases, but it is
            progressively expensive.
          </p>
          <p>
            The abraded backing works against the deck rather than sliding on it, so friction rises
            continuously. The motor draws more current to hold belt speed, and sustained elevated
            current draw is what destroys motors and controller boards. In other words, a $150 part
            you did not replace becomes a $600 repair.
          </p>
          <p>
            A worn belt also tracks less predictably, because the edges are no longer uniform, which
            accelerates its own destruction and can damage the frame edges.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one genuine safety case is delamination. A belt whose layers are separating can fail
            suddenly and unpredictably underfoot. If you can see the running surface lifting away
            from the backing anywhere, stop using the machine until it is replaced — that one is not
            a matter of economics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extending-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Getting Ten Years Instead of Three</h2>
<p>
            Belt life is almost entirely within your control, and the interventions are small.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubricate every 40 to 50 hours of use.</strong> The single largest factor by a
            wide margin. Our
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            covers the timing and the finger test that settles it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check tracking quarterly.</strong> A belt rubbing a side rail loses an edge in
            months, and correcting it is a quarter turn on one bolt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set tension correctly and no tighter.</strong> The lift test: about two to three
            inches at the deck centre.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clean under the belt annually</strong>, six-monthly on carpet, so grit is not
            being dragged around the rollers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use indoor-only shoes.</strong> Free, and it keeps street grit off the running
            surface entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Expect five to ten years</strong> from a maintained belt, and two to three from
            a neglected one. Check it annually while you are cleaning under it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Replace it</strong> when you see a polished centre strip, fraying or curling
            edges, a gritty underside, or slipping that lubrication and correct tension do not fix.
            Replace it <strong>immediately</strong> if the layers are separating anywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assess the deck at the same time</strong>, and flip it if it is reversible and
            worn — the labour overlaps and it doubles deck life for nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Budget $260 to $500</strong> professionally, or the part cost alone if you are
            comfortable with a couple of hours and a helper. And on a budget machine past year six,
            check that figure against a replacement before committing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a treadmill belt be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every five to ten years on a maintained machine, or roughly 1,500 to 3,000 hours of use. A belt that was never lubricated can need replacing in two to three years, because a dry belt-deck interface abrades the backing far faster. Maintenance matters more than mileage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the signs a treadmill belt needs replacing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A polished or thinned strip down the centre where your feet land; fraying or curling at the edges; a rough, gritty feel on the underside; slipping that correct lubrication and tension do not resolve; and — most urgently — any separation of the running surface from the fabric backing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to replace a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The part is $80 to $250, labour $100 to $200, plus an $80 to $150 call-out at many companies — so roughly $260 to $500 done professionally. If the deck also needs replacing, add $100 to $300, though many decks are reversible and flipping one costs only labour.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I replace the deck when I replace the belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Assess it at the same time. A worn belt has been polishing the deck's low-friction laminate for months, and fitting a new belt onto a worn deck wears the new belt prematurely. Many decks are reversible — flipping one gives you a second deck for labour alone, and the machine is already apart.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I replace a treadmill belt myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes for most people. It is mechanically simple and physically awkward rather than technically demanding, and it does not involve the motor compartment or electrical work. Expect two to three hours the first time, and get a second person — the belt is heavy and stiff and even tensioning takes patience.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it dangerous to run on a worn treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually it is expensive rather than dangerous — rising friction makes the motor draw more current, which destroys motors and controller boards. The exception is delamination: a belt whose layers are separating can fail suddenly underfoot. If you can see the surface lifting from the backing, stop using it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Belt Replacement</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two cost you a belt you did not need to buy, and the third turns one repair into two.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing a belt that only needed lubricating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Slipping and hesitation under load are usually friction rather than belt wear. Lubricate properly, set tension with the lift test, and run it for a week before concluding the belt is finished. A great many belts are replaced when a $12 bottle of silicone would have fixed the symptom entirely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting a new belt onto a worn deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A worn belt has spent months polishing away the deck's low-friction laminate. Put a fresh belt on that surface and it wears prematurely, so you buy another sooner than you should. Assess the deck while the machine is apart, and flip it if it is reversible — the labour overlaps almost entirely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a belt by measuring the old one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A fitted belt is under tension and will measure short, and belt dimensions change between model years under the same product name. Use the manufacturer part specification for your exact model and year, and buy from a supplier who lists your model rather than a generic size.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring tracking after fitting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A new belt almost always needs tracking adjustment, and a belt running off-centre rubs the frame or side rails and destroys an edge within months. Run the machine slowly and unloaded after fitting, watch where the belt sits, and correct with quarter turns on one rear roller bolt at a time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

